  "theorem_name": "EvenExponentLeftActiveContaminationClassification",
  "declared_proposition": "Let p and r be odd primes and let m>=1, ell>=1. If p!=3 and p^(2m)+2=r^ell, then (p,m,r,ell)=(5,1,3,3). Consequently the left-active twin prime-power contamination with even exponent and base different from 3 is exactly the single pair (25,27): for every X, L_even,p!=3(X)=1 if X>=25 and 0 otherwise.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Set x=p^m. Because p!=3, x^2+2 is divisible by 3, so the prime-power right side has r=3. If ell=1 then x=1; if ell=2 then x^2=7. For ell>=3, the classical Nagell solution of the Lebesgue-Nagell equation x^2+2=y^ell gives only (x,y,ell)=(5,3,3). Hence p^m=5, so p=5 and m=1. The external Diophantine input is explicitly represented in the proof DAG; the project-local contribution is its exact application to the active contamination identity.",
  "result_classification": "partial_theorem",
